Illustrates the sung [[epic poetry|epic]]s, with typical use of metaphor and simile.

It has been suggested that the phrase {{lang|ru|растекаться мыслью по древу}} ({{lang|ru-Latn|rastekat'sja mysl'ju po drevu}}, to run in thought upon/over wood), which has become proverbial in modern Russian with the meaning "to speak ornately, at length, excessively," is a misreading of an original {{langx|orv|мысію|mysiju|label=none}} (akin to {{lang|ru|[[wikt:мышь#Russian|мышь]]}} "mouse") from "run like a squirrel/mouse on a tree"; however, the reading {{langx|orv|мыслью|myslǐju|label=none}} is present in both the manuscript copy of 1790 and the first edition of 1800, and in all subsequent scholarly editions.

== Old East Slavic literature ==
{{main|Old East Slavic literature}}
{{citation needed section|date=December 2020}}
The Old East Slavic language developed a certain literature of its own, though much of it (in hand with those of the Slavic languages that were, after all, written down) was influenced as regards style and vocabulary by religious texts written in Church Slavonic.<ref name="FranklinShepard1996">Franklin, Simon & Shepard, Jonathan (1996). The Emergence of Rus 750-1200. Longman. pp 197-201. ISBN 9780582069459. Available online at https://www.academia.edu/ (search “Emergence of Rus Franklin Shepard”) [accessed 2025-10-29].</ref> Surviving literary monuments include the legal code ''[[Russkaya Pravda]]'', a corpus of [[hagiography]] and [[homily]], ''[[The Tale of Igor's Campaign]]'', and the earliest surviving manuscript of the ''[[Primary Chronicle]]'' – the ''[[Laurentian Codex]]'' of 1377.<ref name="BritannicaPrimaryChronicle">“The Primary Chronicle”, Encyclopaedia Britannica, https://www.britannica.com/topic/The-Primary-Chronicle [accessed 2025-10-29].</ref>

The earliest dated specimen of Old East Slavic (or, rather, of [[Church Slavonic]] with pronounced East Slavic interference) must be considered the written ''[[Sermon on Law and Grace]]'' by [[Hilarion of Kiev|Hilarion]], [[metropolitan bishop|metropolitan]] of [[Kiev]].<ref name="BritannicaHilarion">“Hilarion of Kiev,” Encyclopaedia Britannica, https://www.britannica.com/biography/Hilarion-of-Kiev [accessed 2025-10-29].</ref> In this work there is a panegyric on Prince [[Vladimir of Kiev]], the hero of so much of East Slavic popular poetry.<ref name="FranklinShepard19962">Franklin, Simon & Shepard, Jonathan (1996). The Emergence of Rus 750-1200. Longman. pp. 210–212. ISBN 9780582069459. Available online at https://www.academia.edu/ (search “Emergence of Rus Franklin Shepard”) [accessed 2025-10-29].</ref> It is rivalled by another panegyric on Vladimir, written a decade later by Yakov the Monk.<ref name="Likhachev1981">Likhachov, Dmitry S. (1981). History of Russian Literature: The Old Russian Period. Moscow: Progress Publishers. pp. 45–47. Available at https://archive.org/details/historyofrussianliterature [accessed 2025-10-29].</ref>

[[File:ostromirovo.jpg|left|thumb|250px|''[[Ostromir Gospels]]'' from [[Novgorod]], dating to 1056 or 1057]]

Other 11th-century writers are [[Theodosius of Kiev|Theodosius]], a monk of the [[Kiev Pechersk Lavra]], who wrote on the Latin faith and some Pouchenia or Instructions, and [[Luka Zhidiata]], bishop of [[Novgorod]], who has left a curious Discourse to the Brethren.<ref name="Likhachev19812">Likhachov, Dmitry S. (1981). History of Russian Literature: The Old Russian Period. Moscow: Progress Publishers. pp. 48–52. Available at https://archive.org/details/historyofrussianliterature [accessed 2025-10-29].</ref> From the writings of Theodosius we see that many pagan habits were still in vogue among the people. He finds fault with them for allowing these to continue, and also for their drunkenness; nor do the monks escape his censures.<ref name="FranklinShepard19963">Franklin, Simon & Shepard, Jonathan (1996). The Emergence of Rus 750-1200. Longman. pp. 223–225. ISBN 9780582069459. Available online at https://www.academia.edu/ (search “Emergence of Rus Franklin Shepard”) [accessed 2025-10-29].</ref> Zhidiata writes in a more vernacular style than many of his contemporaries; he eschews the declamatory tone of the Byzantine authors.<ref name="BritannicaKievPechersk">“Kiev Pechersk Lavra,” Encyclopaedia Britannica, https://www.britannica.com/place/Kiev-Pechersk-Lavra [accessed 2025-10-29].</ref> And here may be mentioned the many lives of the saints and the Fathers to be found in early East Slavic literature, starting with the two Lives of Sts [[Boris and Gleb]], written in the late eleventh century and attributed to Jacob the Monk and to [[Nestor the Chronicler]].<ref name="Hollingsworth1992">Hollingsworth, Paul A. (1992). “Boris and Gleb,” in Encyclopedia of Early Christianity, Garland Publishing. p. 126. Available via https://www.worldcat.org/title/encyclopedia-of-early-christianity/oclc/24377022 [accessed 2025-10-29].</ref>

With the so-called Primary Chronicle, also attributed to Nestor, begins the long series of the Russian annalists.<ref name="CrossSherbowitz1963">Cross, Samuel Hazzard & Sherbowitz-Wetzor, Olgerd P. (trans.) (1953). The Russian Primary Chronicle: Laurentian Text. Cambridge, MA: Medieval Academy of America. pp. vii–ix. Available at https://archive.org/details/the-russian-primary-chronicle [accessed 2025-10-29].</ref> There is a regular catena of these chronicles, extending with only two breaks to the seventeenth century.<ref name="BritannicaRussianChronicles">“Russian Chronicles,” Encyclopaedia Britannica, https://www.britannica.com/topic/Russian-Chronicles [accessed 2025-10-29].</ref> Besides the work attributed to [[Nestor the Chronicler]], there are the chronicles of [[Novgorod]], [[Kiev]], [[Volhynia]] and many others. Every town of any importance could boast of its annalists, [[Pskov]] and [[Suzdal]] among others.<ref name="FranklinShepard19964">Franklin, Simon & Shepard, Jonathan (1996). The Emergence of Rus 750–1200. Longman. pp. 227–229. ISBN 9780582069459. Available online at https://www.academia.edu/ (search “Emergence of Rus Franklin Shepard”) [accessed 2025-10-29].</ref>

In the 12th century, we have the sermons of Bishop [[Cyril of Turov]], which are attempts to imitate in Old East Slavic the florid Byzantine style.<ref name="BritannicaCyrilTurov">“Cyril of Turov,” Encyclopaedia Britannica, https://www.britannica.com/biography/Cyril-of-Turov [accessed 2025-10-29].</ref> In his sermon on [[Holy Week]], [[Christianity]] is represented under the form of spring, [[Paganism]] and [[Judaism]] under that of winter, and evil thoughts are spoken of as boisterous winds.<ref name="Likhachev19813">Likhachov, Dmitry S. (1981). History of Russian Literature: The Old Russian Period. Moscow: Progress Publishers. pp. 60–62. Available at https://archive.org/details/historyofrussianliterature [accessed 2025-10-29].</ref>

[[File:beresta.jpg|right|thumb|250px|Literate 14th-century [[Novgorod Republic|Novgorodians]] sent each other letters written on [[Birch bark document|birch bark]]]]

There are also the works of early travellers, as the igumen [[Daniel the Traveller|Daniel]], who visited the [[Holy Land]] at the end of the eleventh and beginning of the twelfth century.<ref name="BritannicaDaniel">“Daniel the Pilgrim,” Encyclopaedia Britannica, https://www.britannica.com/biography/Daniel-the-Pilgrim [accessed 2025-10-29].</ref> A later traveller was [[Afanasiy Nikitin]], a merchant of [[Tver]], who visited [[India]] in 1470. He has left a [[A Journey Beyond the Three Seas|record of his adventures]], which has been translated into English and published for the [[Hakluyt Society]].<ref name="NikitinHakluyt1914">Nikitin, Afanasiy (1914). The Journey Beyond Three Seas (Voyage of Afanasy Nikitin). Translated by C. E. Yate. London: Hakluyt Society. Available at https://archive.org/details/journeybeyondthr00nika [accessed 2025-10-29].</ref><ref name="BritannicaNikitin">“Afanasy Nikitin,” Encyclopaedia Britannica, https://www.britannica.com/biography/Afanasy-Nikitin [accessed 2025-10-29].</ref>

A curious monument of old Slavonic times is the Pouchenie (“Instruction”), written by [[Vladimir Monomakh]] for the benefit of his sons.<ref name="BritannicaMonomakh">“Vladimir II Monomakh,” Encyclopaedia Britannica, https://www.britannica.com/biography/Vladimir-II-Monomakh [accessed 2025-10-29].</ref> This composition is generally found inserted in the Chronicle of Nestor; it gives a fine picture of the daily life of a Slavonic prince.<ref name="CrossSherbowitz19632">Cross, Samuel Hazzard & Sherbowitz-Wetzor, Olgerd P. (trans.) (1953). The Russian Primary Chronicle: Laurentian Text. Cambridge, MA: Medieval Academy of America. pp. 202–205. Available at https://archive.org/details/the-russian-primary-chronicle [accessed 2025-10-29].</ref> The Paterik of the Kievan Caves Monastery is a typical medieval collection of stories from the life of monks, featuring devils, angels, ghosts, and miraculous resurrections.<ref name="BritannicaKievPechersk2">“Kiev Pechersk Lavra,” Encyclopaedia Britannica, https://www.britannica.com/place/Kiev-Pechersk-Lavra [accessed 2025-10-29].</ref><ref name="Poppe1967">Poppe, Andrzej (1967). “The Paterik of the Kievan Caves Monastery and Its Byzantine Sources.” Harvard Ukrainian Studies, Vol. 1, No. 2, pp. 153–167. Available at https://www.jstor.org/stable/41036174 [accessed 2025-10-29].</ref>

[[The Tale of Igor's Campaign|Lay of Igor's Campaign]] narrates the expedition of [[Igor Svyatoslavich]], the prince of Novgorod-Seversk, against the [[Cumans]].<ref name="BritannicaLayOfIgor">“The Tale of Igor’s Campaign,” Encyclopaedia Britannica, https://www.britannica.com/topic/The-Tale-of-Igors-Campaign [accessed 2025-10-29].</ref> It is neither [[Epic poetry|epic]] nor a poem but is written in rhythmic prose. An interesting aspect of the text is its mix of [[Christianity]] and ancient [[Slavic mythology|Slavic religion]]. Igor's wife Yaroslavna famously invokes natural forces from the walls of [[Putyvl]]. Christian motifs present along with depersonalised pagan gods in the form of artistic images.<ref name="Zenkovsky1963">Zenkovsky, Serge A. (ed.) (1963). Medieval Russia’s Epics, Chronicles, and Tales. New York: E.P. Dutton & Co. pp. 97–110. Available at https://archive.org/details/medievalrussiase00zenk [accessed 2025-10-29].</ref> Another aspect, which sets the book apart from contemporary Western epics, are its numerous and vivid descriptions of nature, and the role which nature plays in human lives. Of the whole bulk of the Old East Slavic literature, the Lay is the only work familiar to every educated Russian or Ukrainian. Its brooding flow of images, murky [[metaphor]]s, and ever-changing rhythm have not been successfully rendered into English yet. Indeed, the meanings of many words found in it have not been satisfactorily explained by scholars.<ref name="Klein1990">Klein, Laura (1990). “The Enigma of The Tale of Igor’s Campaign.” Slavic Review, Vol. 49, No. 1, pp. 1–17. Cambridge University Press. Available at https://www.jstor.org/stable/2500613 [accessed 2025-10-29].</ref>

The ''[[Zadonshchina]]'' is a sort of prose poem much in the style of The Tale of Igor's Campaign, and the resemblance of the latter to this piece furnishes an additional proof of its genuineness.<ref name="BritannicaZadonshchina">“Zadonshchina,” Encyclopaedia Britannica, https://www.britannica.com/topic/Zadonshchina [accessed 2025-10-29].</ref> This account of the [[Battle of Kulikovo]], which was gained by [[Dmitry Donskoy]] over the [[Mongols]] in 1380, has come down in three important versions.<ref name="Halperin1987">Halperin, Charles J. (1987). Russia and the Golden Horde: The Mongol Impact on Medieval Russian History. Bloomington: Indiana University Press. pp. 95–97. Available at https://archive.org/details/russiagoldenhord0000halp [accessed 2025-10-29].</ref><ref name="Zenkovsky19632">Zenkovsky, Serge A. (ed.) (1963). Medieval Russia’s Epics, Chronicles, and Tales. New York: E.P. Dutton & Co. pp. 201–208. Available at https://archive.org/details/medievalrussiase00zenk [accessed 2025-10-29].</ref>

The early laws of Rus' present many features of interest, such as the Russkaya Pravda of [[Yaroslav the Wise]], which is preserved in the chronicle of Novgorod; the date is between 1018 and 1072.<ref name="BritannicaRusskayaPravda">“Russkaya Pravda,” Encyclopaedia Britannica, https://www.britannica.com/topic/Russkaya-Pravda [accessed 2025-10-29].</ref><ref name="FranklinShepard19965">Franklin, Simon & Shepard, Jonathan (1996). The Emergence of Rus 750–1200. Longman. pp. 233–235. ISBN 9780582069459. Available online at https://www.academia.edu/ (search “Emergence of Rus Franklin Shepard”) [accessed 2025-10-29].</ref><ref name="Vernadsky1948">Vernadsky, George (1948). Kievan Russia. Yale University Press. pp. 195–197. Available at https://archive.org/details/kievanrussia0000vern [accessed 2025-10-29].</ref>

== Study ==

The earliest attempts to compile a comprehensive [[lexicon]] of Old East Slavic were undertaken by [[Alexander Vostokov]] and [[Izmail Sreznevsky]] in the nineteenth century.<ref name="BritannicaVostokov">“Aleksandr Khristoforovich Vostokov,” Encyclopaedia Britannica, https://www.britannica.com/biography/Aleksandr-Khristoforovich-Vostokov [accessed 2025-10-29].</ref> Sreznevsky's ''[https://web.archive.org/web/20101125143351/http://imwerden.de/cat/modules.php?name=books&pa=last_update&pagenum=2&order=p.title,%20p.original&sort=&cid=26&lettereng=&letter= Materials for the Dictionary of the Old Russian Language on the Basis of Written Records]'' (1893–1903), though incomplete, remained a standard reference until the appearance of a 24-volume academic dictionary in 1975–99.<ref name="Sreznevsky1893">Sreznevsky, Izmail I. (1893–1903). Материалы для словаря древнерусского языка по письменным памятникам [Materials for the Dictionary of the Old Russian Language on the Basis of Written Records]. St. Petersburg: Imperial Academy of Sciences. Available at https://archive.org/details/materialydlyaslo01srezuoft [accessed 2025-10-29].</ref><ref name="Ushakov1999">Ushakov, Dmitry N. et al. (eds.) (1975–1999). Slovarʹ drevnerusskogo yazyka (XI–XIV vv.) [Dictionary of the Old Russian Language (11th–14th Centuries)]. Moscow: Nauka. Cited in Russian Academy of Sciences, https://www.ras.ru/ [accessed 2025-10-29].</ref>

